92186172105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262764 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262766 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262768 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262734 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262742 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262743 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262758 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262759 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262763 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262765 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168705 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186172312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262362 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172312 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172312 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$F(x, y) = ⟨e^{y} + 2x^{2}y, e^{x} + 2xy^{2}⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 1)$, and $(-1, 1)$." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186172312 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168723 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186172315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262527 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172315 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 2)$, and $(-1, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x, y) = x^{2}yi + 5xyj$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186172315 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186172315 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262698 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262699 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262972 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262973 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262993 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262994 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262995 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262997 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262998 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186175983 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262645 1