#edb/ref 17592186262730 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262731 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262732 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262733 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262735 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262708 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262712 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262717 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262719 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262720 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262714 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262716 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262721 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262722 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262723 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262724 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262725 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262726 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262727 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262728 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262729 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262740 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262741 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262744 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262745 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262746 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186179752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262747 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177855 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262557 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180036 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = xi + 5j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25x^{2} + y^{2} = 25$." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180036 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+5\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180036 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{cos}^{2}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177867 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262249 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180038 1071 "![](images/77/772added7ce46ba0ec25de66dfd03e0107787d851006f261ac8843eba008a2d2.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n2. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180038 1071 "![](images/77/772added7ce46ba0ec25de66dfd03e0107787d851006f261ac8843eba008a2d2.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180038 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177879 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262393 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180040 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i - y^{2}j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$. If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$∮_{C}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n = xi + yj$.*" 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180040 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180040 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177885 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262356 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180041 1071 "![](images/b3/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i + y^{2}j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side $C_{1}$ is given by\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180041 1071 "![](images/b3/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180041 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177891 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262127 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180042 1071 "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = 2j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180042 1071 "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180042 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177903 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262524 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180044 1071 "![](images/c7/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = xi + yj$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t) = ti + t^{2}j$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C$, as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180044 1071 "![](images/c7/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180044 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/e9/e90c36e34c6ab51f15707685a22c8bd2c749b9857ca24c59631f09dfbda495c6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t)\\cdot (2t)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177909 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262421 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180045 1071 "![](images/27/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y^{2} = 4x$ from the point $(0, 0)$ to the point $(1, 2)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = 2i - 4j$ through $C$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C$, as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186180045 1071 "![](images/27/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\,i-4\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186180045 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2.)$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/b7/b7ac1ce4a79528f5660b8f898739da72489ebe8e8fc156020efae77a103fbec4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2)\\cdot (1)-(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262615 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262616 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262617 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262618 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262619 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262664 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262665 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262666 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186192760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262582 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186192760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262583 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186192760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262584 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186192760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262585 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186192760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262586 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186203904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262841 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186204197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186199692 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186204197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262301 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186204197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186204197 1071 "Find $(7a - 2b) \\cdot b$, if $b \\cdot a = ⟨2,-1, 6⟩$." 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186204197 1071 "Find $(7a-2b)\\times b$, if $b\\times a=⟨2,-1,6⟩.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186204197 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n(7a-2b)\\times b=(7a)\\times b-(2b)\\times b\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n(7a)\\times b-(2b)\\times b=7(a\\times b)-2(b\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $b\\times b=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(a\\times b)-2(b\\times b) & =7(-b\\times a)-2(0) \\\\  & =-7(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(b\\times a) & =-7⟨2,-1,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-14,7,-42⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186233383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262279 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186233383 1074 "![](images/51/51bf87385bc52632a0bf722d5672047e79afc8ea0556dabda3257c9ebd1e91a3.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186233383 1074 "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 false] [17592186233386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262214 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259878 1081 "z=x-y" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259878 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259878 1084 #uuid "001b1c11-d1f2-5243-9113-be59a42fe479" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259879 1081 "-24\\pi " 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259879 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259879 1084 #uuid "002f13da-1ead-583a-ac5e-651659fba636" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259880 1081 "\\frac{1}{14}" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259880 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186259880 1084 #uuid "002fdf74-