$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262685 1072 #uuid "e6ec21a6-8f59-5caa-a4ef-419db2a6cedd" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262685 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=\\pi x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\pi x}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{2n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{\\pi x}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\pi x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262349 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262686 1069 "q-126991" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262686 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-1,0,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262686 1072 #uuid "93d8d751-1660-5c0d-a647-5382ac8f5fb7" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262686 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩)-⟨3,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{5},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |⟨-t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-t)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+0+4{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,1⟩+⟨-1,0,2⟩=⟨2,1,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,1⟩-⟨-1,0,2⟩=⟨4,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,-1).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262435 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262687 1069 "q-126992" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262687 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,-2,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-2,1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262687 1072 #uuid "01976ddb-a3c2-5d6a-8e10-0f4fa56cdf01" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262687 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩)-⟨3,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩+⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨1,-1,5⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩-⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨5,-3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262338 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262688 1069 "q-126996" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262688 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{4}-2t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262688 1072 #uuid "7411fcce-d4c2-5543-ad5f-e1792bed18cc" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262688 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}-2t \\\\ 3+t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -2t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262434 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262689 1069 "q-126997" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262689 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3 \\\\ y=5t \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262689 1072 #uuid "66b1db3e-6731-578f-a2e5-78774370e2fe" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262689 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5t \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262531 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262690 1069 "q-127001" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262690 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=11 \\\\ y=3+7t \\\\ z=-5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262690 1072 #uuid "a1e93a97-3511-5288-840e-250fb655dbc1" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262690 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3+7t \\\\ -5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7t \\\\ -5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262248 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262691 1069 "q-127013" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262691 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262691 1072 #uuid "22f3b282-003f-570b-9433-d53609b5be7e" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262691 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262523 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262692 1069 "q-127014" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262692 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\n- $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$\n- $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262692 1072 #uuid "b52094f1-1c52-540b-aa05-57b1e1d10dfd" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262692 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function I satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262542 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262693 1069 "q-127025" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262693 1071 "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262693 1072 #uuid "18437e48-5bf8-597d-848c-dabff126da0e" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262693 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262250 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262694 1069 "q-127029" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262694 1071 "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262694 1072 #uuid "e84dd576-6f5a-58c5-bf96-4c1a434830ab" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262694 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262262 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262695 1069 "q-12703" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262695 1071 "Calculate $\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262695 1072 #uuid "e9224047-f3da-5bf1-a76d-027468545ef6" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262695 1074 "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2})(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{10{e}^{3x}}{27}(3x-1)-\\frac{10D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3x}}{27}(9{x}^{2}-6x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{10D}{3}.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262163 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262696 1069 "q-127030" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262696 1071 "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262696 1072 #uuid "92477238-fcf4-57c0-a788-d4cbd2b1a3bc" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262696 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262539 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262697 1069 "q-127032" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262697 1071 "![](images/45/453633f6309b9f1d2a3f7a8d4266ff10f571823cffd705d0bc93b930dce63d8f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262697 1072 #uuid "6542e3fc-27ed-5a91-abb0-bbb5f848f669" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262697 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/84/844d5e5574571ae221a86f541820a4b215766451031f4eea678cee46a1b83e41.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b31d46646db2d88d8a3f5658ee876383795ad5ba9aa55eb9959d5f95c02dbe.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262239 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262698 1069 "q-127037" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262698 1071 "![](images/e9/e97552a634b4e3ad3604de9d75742af05aa30a18ee92c8440e6c3cedd5d7273a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[0,4]$\n- $[1,6]$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262698 1072 #uuid "82be4976-c19d-544a-b202-bf6dca5175c8" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262698 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-4,-1]$ is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,4]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=0.$ So, it can't be continuous at this point. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,6]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,6].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262436 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262699 1069 "q-127040" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262699 1071 "![](images/3c/3c25115cfb7ccde7ec0cbec249d62b037c7fb94be92905b186101ce1fd45a1d8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $(1,2)$\n- $(-\\infty ,0]$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262699 1072 #uuid "144d9fbc-7660-5ed9-9ae4-01d5a04d6a5a" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262699 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\infty ,0]$ is closed, but it is not a bounded interval in the form $[a,b].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533399 