e{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0-12t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(0-0)-12(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-24\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262157 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262726 1069 "q-127148" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262726 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=8\\,i-(x+3)\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262726 1072 #uuid "4b077b66-1e99-5133-b08d-623645791b05" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262726 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $2.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =8, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+3=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for sine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262192 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262727 1069 "q-127149" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262727 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-5\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262727 1072 #uuid "5660b13f-68bb-56f7-8d7c-83ccf48f3c64" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262727 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $5.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =25(0+\\pi +1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =25\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262384 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262728 1069 "q-127151" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262728 1071 "![](images/0e/0ea0204d3e1f230efcde4b82da36b1986af732cdf2a01ad868aa0f6bf7414326.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4y\\,i+(2x-1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262728 1072 #uuid "42e5f742-bcfa-5cc0-b6a7-83f64e545deb" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262728 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/59/59e503e0c4d950f030891bbaeb7ed890426ef470a1e1dae89d27eabb8dd591a6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4y=4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x-1=2{t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2}-1)\\cdot (2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t-4{t}^{3}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t-4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-{t}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-1)-(0-0){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262470 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262729 1069 "q-127153" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262729 1071 "![](images/fb/fbfe241043e9014c1b633d163cf7e06234db70702a9052297b5df8b9df3fd82f.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262729 1072 #uuid "22f56e61-1496-5d08-b396-c18e68744d11" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262729 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/d4/d432857e3350dfcb70a2a00b66ad9b86bc207382edd277c2169be0834c1710e0.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}\\frac{1}{t}+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262289 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262730 1069 "q-127190" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262730 1071 "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262730 1072 #uuid "b8d84a8f-b961-5115-a51c-b62b1cb780d8" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262730 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262212 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262731 1069 "q-127194" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262731 1071 "![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262731 1072 #uuid "622ab5d8-9ae6-5da5-96af-e733a10932b2" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262731 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262313 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262732 1069 "q-127198" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262732 1071 "![](images/fd/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262732 1072 #uuid "d9184553-8293-59d4-95a8-2cc25c169eab" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262732 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262223 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262733 1069 "q-127200" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262733 1071 "![](images/0b/0b652e9eae16ae78a06743e75513674cc6d7596aadf2bd07aca4398a99089348.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262733 1072 #uuid "2de65327-4951-549c-b9fb-03c2c7cc11a7" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262733 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262375 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262734 1069 "q-127206" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262734 1071 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262734 1072 #uuid "c8fd9f34-b5c0-5dde-8e43-965eb6f71e26" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262734 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/98/98d7da0c7cd1e1f1006c1db74ae1a1e1f198d9066df6585745ee36548f0b8d96.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2xy.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262415 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262735 1069 "q-127207" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262735 1071 "![](images/66/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262735 1072 #uuid "b49bddc7-bed1-5bef-8ddb-d1f67820d61a" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262735 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262178 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262736 1069 "q-127211" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262736 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+5t\\,j$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262736 1072 #uuid "2b8296dd-e335-5dab-af3a-2fe54c8ea8ad" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262736 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+25}}(2t\\,i+5\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262225 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262737 1069 "q-127212" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262737 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262737 1072 #uuid "a1e84cf8-bc13-580b-9bc4-045d64ca23f8" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262737 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),\\,y(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262564 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262738 1069 "q-127213" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262738 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+(2-t)\\,j$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262738 1072 #uuid "3887dd3a-81c5-5f4c-a731-be5686af8db3" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262738 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+4t}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+4t}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}i-\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262165 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262739 1069 "q-127219" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262739 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262739 1072 #uuid "2400fe5d-0246-5c80-9323-e363a0931049" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262739 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,\\,2(1),\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262574 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262740 1069 "q-127223" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262740 1071 "![](images/41/414611a90c2227ff163d40a4f6f560bc67c471fa0f35724e459cec280e4904f9.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4\\,i+2\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2t}\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262740 1072 #uuid "d9d8ed81-5fd6-581e-8bd7-3dd3517c5bfe" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262740 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/11/11376e6ef18b151a6dd1a02dd09f20b72bce3160c718f4095b4f08b23721ff1f.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2t})=-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(4)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}})-(2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{t}-t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)-(4-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262134 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262741 1069 "q-127227" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262741 1071 "![](images/85/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262741 1072 #uuid "1caebf8b-fbe2-5515-a0df-5bb50a3ced16" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262741 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/55/559bbc20aa7d47eaad47a8975f80c8efb91bd03bc816357bc6047675dc755c9b.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =5{e}^{-x}=5{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\frac{5}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{5}{t})\\cdot (1)-(4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262379 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262742 1069 "q-127229" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262742 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}\\,i+6x{y}^{4}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262742 1072 #uuid "64b95036-f7c8-5305-8593-d176862393c3" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262742 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =24x{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{3}+24x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262284 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262743 1069 "q-127231" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262743 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-3,-1),(-1,-1),(-1,1),$ and $(-3,1),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x)\\,i+(-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4})\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262743 1072 #uuid "25b638ef-851a-5e05-bca0-4d63723c7d75" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262743 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/54/5497f23e51d1e745fef1cf39d6c1a8ada22b25e8c9c7b87f5d5338e85a941739.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2yx+{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2yx+4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2yx+{y}^{3})+(-2yx+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262208 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262744 1069 "q-127233" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262744 1071 "![](images/9b/9bb15934c271a17851342b9032f5096f48e7ac9ee4e149af92ac1010dea46d88.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i+(2x+1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262744 1072 #uuid "d7df129a-7685-5a03-8c7b-340092011e9b" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262744 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/0e/0e751adb9f44d24ba89af318fe5f063b1be146bbae45d05fc2dd9a99bef259f2.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3xy=3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x+1=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2})(1)-(2t+1)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2t-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1-1)-(0-0-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262200 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262745 1069 "q-127244" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262745 1071 "![](images/60/6043a1f88474e2c282938d3b3f0193002b6e11b5fb6d16292fea43d63292d1b0.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y={x}^{3}$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-3{x}^{2}\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262745 1072 #uuid "b74be94c-a6f2-5d0a-8660-699d2ff2d45b" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262745 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/1d/1d54fe3c07f04a7d05567942e3eea6efaf1cb516070a5d7cc31ef726c0ddb423.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-3{x}^{2}=-3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3{t}^{2})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{t}^{4}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{5}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262215 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262746 1069 "q-127253" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262746 1071 "![](images/cd/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262746 1072 #uuid "42dae85e-af3c-52f4-a342-edbfb5509ac9" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262746 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/e2/e288ed1095be61e4645fb63c22b7fd2cb605d34795c30557e84afb31b7ec8721.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1)\\cdot (-1)-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262445 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262747 1069 "q-127254" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262747 1071 "![](images/67/67dca8f0202d0c5d1041935a6aee7c4ebd0e3c6c449f2ee1a5ff24c1f381410c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262747 1072 #uuid "6e7eff5f-8b84-5752-bf2b-9206857b0d37" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262747 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{e},1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/08/08fc4561a322b690da7bd28a9e69a91c687256e8a902ec7690dda5e6a1c689a6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =0, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2{y}^{2}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=-\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(0)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}){|}_{1/e}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262348 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262748 1069 "q-127260" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262748 1071 "The absolute difference between the outcomes of one roll of two fair tetrahedral dice follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the absolute difference of the outcomes of one roll of the two dice?\n\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262748 1072 #uuid "a63919d9-349d-5f1c-bd71-0f6ab200fe95" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262748 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{4})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3+4+3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262501 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262749 1069 "q-127267" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262749 1071 "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262749 1072 #uuid "b99393d9-732e-53ca-adce-90aad99a794a" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262749 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12+4-12 \\\\ -4-5+4 \\\\ -2+4+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262513 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262750 1069 "q-127269" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262750 1071 "The absolute difference between the outcomes of one roll of two fair six-sided dice follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the absolute difference of the outcomes for one roll of the two dice?\n\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ | $\\frac{4}{18}$ | $\\frac{3}{18}$ | $\\frac{2}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262750 1072 #uuid "e811f3df-a27f-5fdf-9386-dd1c84fc9cd9" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262750 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{5}{18})+2(\\frac{4}{18})+3(\\frac{3}{18})+4(\\frac{2}{18})+5(\\frac{1}{18}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{8}{18}+\\frac{9}{18}+\\frac{8}{18}+\\frac{5}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+5+8+9+8+5}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262268 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262751 1069 "q-127274" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262751 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, given that  $p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{3}\\,k.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262751 1072 #uuid "98372d07-efe7-5b21-9b1e-f8ccc9309af9" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262751 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2(i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)+(1)(\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)+\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+12} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262259 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262752 1069 "q-127276" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262752 1071 "Find $Mz$ if \n$M=[\\begin{aligned}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$z=[\\begin{aligned}s \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262752 1072 #uuid "e074f846-d8be-5da0-81fe-94cca19214d0" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262752 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Mz & =[\\begin{aligned}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}s \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}s\\cdot s+t\\cdot t \\\\ (-t)\\cdot s+s\\cdot t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ -st+st\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262311 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262753 1069 "q-127277" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262753 1071 "Find $Qg$ if \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$g=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262753 1072 #uuid "81468501-6833-55ff-88ed-8291ef6d3a80" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262753 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Qg & =[\\begin{aligned}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}m\\cdot (-2)+2m\\cdot 3 \\\\ -m\\cdot (-2)+n\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2m+6m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262169 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262754 1069 "q-127280" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262754 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, given that  $p(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,k$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-2\\,i-8\\,j+4\\,k.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262754 1072 #uuid "40eb5610-e5b9-5e89-9b56-cc5f1ae5cba9" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262754 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})p(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}){p}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,i-8\\,j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =(3\\,i+3\\,k)+(-\\,i-4\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j+5\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{r}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot (2\\,i-4\\,j+5\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\,i-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n $T(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\,i-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262395 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262755 1069 "q-127288" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262755 1071 "If $S=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand $T=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ then $ST=$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262755 1072 #uuid "75afaf68-18f2-5156-92d7-f2f5ab33affb" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262755 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-6) & (-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-6) & 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+12 & -4+8 \\\\ 12+(-6) & 12+(-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262572 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262756 1069 "q-127290" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262756 1071 "If $L=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$\nand\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$\nthen $LM=$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262756 1072 #uuid "4c389436-1705-55b7-8a0f-be23fbdf52db" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262756 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-36)+7\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-36)+3\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+21 & 2+7 \\\\ -9+9 & 1+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262448 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262757 1069 "q-127297" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262757 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(t+{t}^{3})p(t)$ at the point where $t=1$, if $p(1)=\\,i-2\\,j+k$ and ${p}^{′}(1)=-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262757 1072 #uuid "8ef07c4d-4b5b-5a62-b1ba-4c5e16bcb308" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262757 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+{t}^{3})p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{3})p(t)+(t+{t}^{3})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =(1+3{t}^{2})p(t)+(t+{t}^{3}){p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =(1+3(1)^{2})p(1)+(1+{1}^{3}){p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =4(i-2\\,j+k)+2(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-6j+8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(-6\\,j+8\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=1$ is $T(1)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\,k.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262497 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262758 1069 "q-127303" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262758 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262758 1072 #uuid "db5d90ba-c5db-5d34-b26f-640edea7636c" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262758 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35e378bca35f4b0822c713556065415d59b5c566ec8fcc8e3124256aac7b7f14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{5}-3y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-(-1))\\,dy \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262347 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262759 1069 "q-127308" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262759 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3},\\,x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262759 1072 #uuid "4fb71543-2fec-5ce1-b3ae-7f7da8898b87" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262759 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/2696cdadd3cb020cfbe343b9c372893ac18b63f3548ff71003b7328b19de39d5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-3{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(-3(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-(-3(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6)\\,dy \\\\  & =-6y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262213 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262760 1069 "q-127309" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262760 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262760 1072 #uuid "d270431a-39a1-5ea3-a306-51d0cd90b607" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262760 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6e80e3010ec7c40d3f59dbb1da4e77c985c889a720b6f954177d8ad83a01351.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12yx,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262547 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262761 1069 "q-127316" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262761 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x-\\sqrt{y},\\,y-\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262761 1072 #uuid "b1199c00-6bec-5b54-8e65-8c9cf313406e" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262761 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262392 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262762 1069 "q-127319" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262762 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262762 1072 #uuid "cc3b5472-625f-54f7-8fb7-e79535f4fbcc" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262762 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨2,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =i \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,0⟩| \\\\  & =|2+0+0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262135 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262763 1069 "q-127322" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262763 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262763 1072 #uuid "5702cf64-a04c-533d-bb19-d90253b29fec" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262763 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262176 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262764 1069 "q-127323" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262764 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262764 1072 #uuid "235350dc-4663-567f-92d6-1215c91e7efd" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262764 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/3a/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262378 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262765 1069 "q-127324" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262765 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262765 1072 #uuid "d93ec47e-8065-5376-b8dd-7b5786ea175a" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262765 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262563 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262766 1069 "q-127325" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262766 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262766 1072 #uuid "0a994fc8-218e-56c0-8e85-b472da8df87e" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262766 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-(-\\frac{y}{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262444 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262767 1069 "q-127335" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262767 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262767 1072 #uuid "0386dac5-9403-597b-be20-d67f4b02e9a9" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262767 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =‖⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(1)(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262440 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262768 1069 "q-127365" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262768 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨30xy,\\,15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y,$ $\\,x=-\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262768 1072 #uuid "e551ea97-e0b9-53f0-8487-f26c11c3ea32" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262768 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/24/24369439bc62cf06084a43d97dff0cee4e4089fd103511ef73d9dd5af1256ebb.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=30xy$ and $Q(x,y)=15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=30y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =30y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(30y+30y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}60y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y[x]_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y+\\sqrt{y})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+{y}^{3/2})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =60(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262193 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262769 1069 "q-127386" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262769 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262769 1072 #uuid "aa7753ff-2008-5279-a3f6-0e08907f2bc4" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262769 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\  & =3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} & =\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡3x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-0.630\\,85$ to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.416\\,35$ to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-0.630\\,85,\\,x≈0.416\\,35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-0.630\\,85$ and $x≈0.416\\,35.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are ${x}_{1}≈-0.630\\,85$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.416\\,35.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.630\\,85\\cdot 0.416\\,35≈-0.26\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262330 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262770 1069 "q-127390" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262770 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262770 1072 #uuid "35c86c6f-7499-5b1c-bbcb-382db4912a2a" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262770 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262305 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262771 1069 "q-127391" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262771 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262771 1072 #uuid "13dc6cf8-4247-5345-8915-7e98d8dd8d84" 13194139533399 17592186045585 true] [17592186262771 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\p